  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 223 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Despite the dewpoint depressions, light winds, clear \nskies, and surface high pressure, the radiational cooling has not \nbeen very pronounced. There's still some time before high clouds and \nwarm advection begin, but it seems we'll not fall as low as \nforecast. Temperatures are still plenty cool with 20s across the\nregion. A compact vort will track northeast. Clouds will \noverspread the region much of the day, and there should be some \nsigns of virga. A very dry layer from the surface-850mb will \nlikely prevent any precipitation from reaching the ground. \nForecast soundings suggest precipitation should make it to about\n5000-6000 ft elevation before evaporating/sublimating away. \nOverall, a somewhat dreary, but not a bad weather day as \ntemperatures climb into the 50s with light south winds.\n\nTonight into Thursday, those south winds will increase ahead of an \nincoming frontal boundary. South winds overnight will mean that the \nChamplain Valley and northern slopes of the Adirondacks will be \nwarmest with low to mid 40s, while the rest of the region should \nstill fall in and around the freezing mark. The same areas will \nlikely also observe the strongest gusts by mid afternoon, likely \nranging from 25-35 mph. Temperatures will respond with the steady \nsouth to southwest winds, and climb into the 60s, perhaps even \ntouching 70 F. NBM probabilities range 10-50%, and the forecast \nexpressly has 70 F for areas like Fort Covington, Mooers, and \nAltona, New York.\n\nFor the front, there are no significant changes. A modest frontal \nboundary will shift southeast. Recent dryness will make its narrow \nmoisture feed unlikely to produce significant precipitation as \nsynoptic support falters, and the frontal boundary stretches out. So \nthere will be a northwest-southeast \"gradient\" where northern areas \nreceive a few hundredths, and areas to the south receive little to \nno precipitation. More seasonable temperatures into the 50s in the \nday and 20s-lower 30s at night return behind the front. \nPostfrontal gusts will be strongest on Friday with good \nafternoon mixing bringing gusty northwest winds of 20-30 mph, \nlocally to 35 mph for areas like Ellenburg, NY.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Surface high pressure will build over the region for the \nweekend, bringing another period of dry and seasonable weather. High \ntemperatures will generally climb into the 50s and low 60s this \nweekend. Heading into next week, it looks there could be a few \nchances for showers towards the start of next week, although some of \nthe latest global deterministic models suggest the dry pattern we've \nbeen in will continue into early next week. We'll have to keep and \neye on trends given the large amount of uncertainty at this stage.",
